# Lab 21-1: Add Service to a Firewall

## Objective

As `user1` with `sudo` privileges on `server30`, permanently allow HTTPS traffic through the default firewalld zone. Verify that the rule was added to the zone configuration and that firewalld recognizes the service.

## Steps

First, verify the current default zone:

```bash
sudo firewall-cmd --get-default-zone
```

Add HTTPS as a permanent service rule to the default zone:

```bash
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=public --add-service=https
```

Reload firewalld so the permanent configuration becomes active:

```bash
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
```

Verify that HTTPS is now listed as an allowed service:

```bash
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-services
```

The output should include:

```text
https
```

The persistent zone configuration can also be viewed directly:

```bash
sudo cat /etc/firewalld/zones/public.xml
```

The XML file should contain an HTTPS service entry similar to:

```xml
<service name="https"/>
```

## Result

HTTPS traffic was permanently added to the default `public` zone. The configuration was activated with `firewall-cmd --reload`, and the rule was verified both through `firewall-cmd` and the zone's XML configuration file.

